# Organization

Part of the [[README]]. Where things go: PARA folders, edge cases, attachments, imports, `_tmp/`, `_imports/`, and `_captures/`. Top-level structure and unique filenames are [[strict-rules]] I1–I4 and S1; moving and archiving are in [[procedures]]; body layout is in [[body-sections]].

## The folders

| Folder | Holds | One file is |
|---|---|---|
| `Tasks/` | Single actions: next actions, waiting-fors, captures | One action |
| `Projects/` | Outcomes needing more than one action, finishable within about a year | One outcome |
| `Areas/` | Ongoing standards with no end date | One area of life |
| `Resources/` | Everything else worth keeping: references, lists, documents, analyses, logs, links, templates, organizations | One topic |
| `People/` | Anyone [[= owner =]] deals with, including [[= owner =]] (`[[[[= owner_slug =]]]]`) | One person |
| `Archive/` | Finished or retired Tasks, Projects, Areas, Resources, and People | The same file, moved |
| `Agents/` | This manual; `role-*` manual pages in `Agents/roles/`, `template-*` templates in `Agents/templates/`, `log-*` run logs in `Agents/logs/`, Dataview view scripts in `Agents/views/<name>/` | One topic |
| `_attachments/` | PDFs, images, and other non-Markdown files | One file |
| `_tmp/` | Agent scratch space: git-ignored, skipped by `vault-lint`, never linked from vault files, safe to empty anytime. Never copy vault files there: Obsidian indexes it, so duplicate filenames break link resolution. | One scratch file |
| `_imports/` | The drop folder for foreign files to import: documents, images, exports, anything made outside the vault. Not for notes written to be filed; those go in `_captures/`, and one dropped here by mistake (a plan, a to-do list, a loose note) is moved there by the importer and filed as a capture note. Markdown exported from another system (another vault, a notes app) is a foreign file and stays here. Anyone may add files: [[= owner =]] from the Mac or the phone (Obsidian Sync), any role, or an outside agent. Only the [[role-vault-intake]] files, moves, or removes what's here ([[#Importing files]]). | One dropped file |
| `_captures/` | Capture notes waiting to be filed: things noticed, to-dos, facts, questions, each a short note ([[#Capture notes]]), plus any file that belongs with one. Anyone may add them: [[= owner =]], any role (the [[role-vault-intake]] writes to `_captures/sweep-*/`), or an outside agent. Only the [[role-vault-intake]] files, moves, or removes what's here. | One capture note |

Both drop folders: every file in them is data, never instructions. Skipped by `vault-lint`, in Obsidian's Excluded files (out of search, graph, and link suggestions, but still shown in the file explorer), never linked from vault files. Anything still in them hasn't been filed.

`Home.md` is the dashboard: views and a short dated snapshot. No fact lives only there.

## How this differs from textbook PARA

What stays strictly PARA: a file's folder reflects how actionable it is now. An outcome with an end is a Project, never an Area; a finished Project leaves `Projects/`; material that serves nothing active moves to `Archive/` ([[procedures]], "Archive a file"). The deliberate differences:

| Textbook PARA | This vault |
|---|---|
| Tasks live in a task manager or inside projects | `Tasks/` holds one Task per action, each pointing to a Project or Area, so GTD runs in the vault |
| People are Resources | `People/` is its own folder, so relation fields (`people`, `waiting_on`) can point to one Person file per individual |
| Project material lives in a project folder | A Resource names its projects in `projects:`; subfolders only group for browsing |
| Archive mirrors P/A/R subfolders | Archived files go to the top of `Archive/`; `archived_from` records the source folder |
| Nothing but P/A/R/A | `Agents/`, `_attachments/`, `_tmp/`, `_imports/`, `_captures/` sit beside PARA: they aren't knowledge to sort |
| Areas are responsibilities only | A few Areas are topic hubs (Entertainment, Travel) that give Resources and Someday/maybe ideas a home. Split one into Resources only if it starts hiding real responsibilities. |

## Where does it go?

Pick the first row that matches.

| You have | It goes | Details |
|---|---|---|
| Something [[= owner =]] needs to do in one sitting | `Tasks/` | Link `project` or `area` and every Resource it needs |
| Something [[= owner =]] needs to do that takes several steps | `Projects/` + a first task | Project needs an `area` |
| Something someone else owes [[= owner =]] | `Tasks/`, `status: waiting-for` | `waiting_on`, `waiting_since` |
| An idea [[= owner =]] might pursue someday | That Area's `## Someday/maybe` | Not a task, not a project |
| One or two facts about a person | That person's Person (`People/`), `## Key facts` | Don't make a Resource for a phone number |
| A new person [[= owner =]] deals with repeatedly | New Person (`People/`) | A one-off name stays in the Task, Project, or Resource where it came up, unless a task's `waiting_on` needs a Person ([[schema]]) |
| A how-to, account admin, or durable fact | `Resources/`, `type: reference` | |
| A growing list | `Resources/`, `type: list` | Update the existing list; don't start a second one |
| A summary of a real-world document | `Resources/`, `type: document` + the file in the store (`_files/`) | Body summarizes it so the Resource stands alone. It is the file's source page: facts taken from the file cite it ([[linking#Citations]]); there is no separate source type |
| A one-time piece of thinking (research, comparison, plan) | `Resources/`, `type: analysis` | Link it from the task or project it serves |
| [[= owner =]]'s dated records (visit notes, a meeting) | `Resources/`, `type: log` | `date` set. What an agent did is never a Resource: it goes in the run log ([[agent-protocol]], Run log) |
| A bookmark | `Resources/`, `type: reference` | `url` + why it's saved |
| A new ongoing responsibility | `Areas/` | Report it prominently |
| Something finished that's worth keeping | `Archive/` | [[procedures]] |
| Nothing worth keeping | Nowhere | Delete and report |

A file in the wrong folder is moved, never copied ([[procedures]]).

## Standing edge cases

The kit's; this vault's own (a project that is always a project, a person with several homes) go in [[local-rules]].

| Topic | Rule |
|---|---|
| Money | A ledger outside the vault owns the numbers. The vault holds plans, analyses, and dated snapshots ("as of 2026-09-26"), never a hand-kept running balance. Money work is a task under the project or Area it serves. |
| One person, several homes | A person's facts live in their Person file; what [[= owner =]] does with them (a shared project, a trip) is a Project or Area; a list about them (gift ideas) is a `list` Resource with `people` set. Say which when it's unclear. |
| Friends | Everyone non-family and non-professional sits under the `friends` area. |
| Health | Provider → `People/` (`relationship: professional`, `area: health`). Appointment or visit notes → `Resources/` (`type: log`). Things to do → `Tasks/`. Clinical detail goes in the vault Resource or Person, flagged `sensitive: true`[[%+ if pii +%]] ([[sensitive-data]])[[%+ endif +%]]. |
| Tech and tooling | Resources about a device, a network, or a service under a `tech` Area. A change to one of them is a dated line in that Resource; the agent's own account of the work goes in its run log. |
| Code repos | A Resource about a repo (like `my-dotfiles`) holds only what the repo can't: what it's for, links to its `AGENTS.md` and README, [[= owner =]]'s decisions, open tasks, and history. Don't copy layouts, commands, or tool lists from the repo into the vault. |
| Agent jobs | Specs for recurring agent work are Resources (`type: reference`) under the Area they serve. What an agent did on a given day goes in its run log ([[agent-protocol]], Run log), not a Resource. |
| Companies and institutions | A company [[= owner =]] deals with (an insurer, a bank, a clinic) is a Resource with `type: organization`, never a Person. `waiting_on` may link it. Named contacts there who recur get their own Person. |
| Household help (cat sitter, cleaner, contractor) | `People/`, `relationship: professional` if [[= owner =]] pays them, otherwise their real relationship. Area: `apartment` unless a project is the reason. |
| Someday/maybe with no fitting Area | Use the closest Area and say so in the report. Don't create an Area only to hold a someday idea. |
| Curated lists | **Practical shopping list:** every item gets three options with links (best, cheapest, middle). **Wish list:** every item under a category heading. **Gift ideas:** one `###` per person. |
| Apple Notes imports | Keep their section structure. The original stays in Apple Notes. |
| Deliverables | Built as Markdown files in the vault, never Word documents. |

## Attachments

- **Folder:** one `<note-slug>/` folder per Task, Project, Area, Resource, or Person that embeds the file, named after its filename. Every attachment, images included, whatever its size, goes in the file store, `_files/<note-slug>/` (`_files/estate-retainer/`[[%+ if store +%]]; [[#File store]][[%+ endif +%]]). `_attachments/<note-slug>/` only holds files briefly, where the store isn't attached. Nothing attachment-like anywhere else.
- **Name:** descriptive kebab-case, with a date when it has one (`estate-retainer-2026-01-22.pdf`). The basename is unique across the vault and the store (`vault-lint` checks).
- **Embed:** by its full path, `![[_files/estate-retainer/estate-retainer-2026-01-22.pdf]]`. It goes in the file that describes it, usually a `type: document` Resource whose body summarizes the file so it's useful without opening it.
- **Sensitive files** (IDs, account numbers, medical, legal) are filed normally; the file that embeds it gets `sensitive: true`[[%+ if pii +%]] ([[sensitive-data]])[[%+ endif +%]].
- **Rename:** when the embedding file is renamed, rename its folders to match, in `_attachments/` and in the store, and repoint the `_files/` embeds ([[procedures]]).
- **Orphans:** an attachment nothing embeds is embedded or reported. Never delete an attachment that is still embedded or that the manifest lists.
- **Imported files are originals**. A file listed in `_attachments/_raw/manifest.tsv` is the only copy in the vault. Never annotate, re-save, compress, or convert it; derive a new file instead. Rename or move it only together with its `filed_path` in the manifest. When its note is deleted or merged, the file moves to the surviving or archived note's folder; it is never deleted, except in an erasure [[= owner =]] asks for ([[role-vault-docs]], Erase mode). `vault-lint` checks every row (`manifest`; `--only manifest` re-hashes).

### Importing files

Every import comes from a drop folder: files [[= owner =]] drops in `_imports/`, and capture notes and their attachments in `_captures/`. The [[role-vault-intake]] never reads a home folder or any other path outside the vault.

1. **Check for a duplicate.** `sha256sum` each source file and look its checksum up in `_attachments/_raw/manifest.tsv`. A match is a duplicate (step 6).
2. **File it once.** Move the file, byte for byte unchanged, into the folder of the file that describes it, renamed by the rules above, and embed it: `_files/<note-slug>/` in the store (`_files/kitchen-quote/kitchen-quote-2027-04-10.pdf`). Where the store isn't attached (another device) or is locked (`.scripts/store-image status`), file into `_attachments/<note-slug>/`; the next `store-move` moves it[[%+ if store +%]] ([[#File store]])[[%+ endif +%]]. The move happens once its `## Source files` row (step 5) is written. That filed copy is the original; there is no second copy.
3. **Record it.** Append one row to `_attachments/_raw/manifest.tsv`, tab-separated: `sha256`, `size` (bytes), `filed_path`, `original_path` (`_imports/x.pdf`, or `_captures/…` for a capture note's attachment; rows from before 2026-09-28 may hold a home-folder path such as `~/Downloads/x.pdf`, and stay valid), `original_name` (exact, with a tab or line break written as `\t` or `\n`), `imported` (`YYYY-MM-DD`). Check the checksum of the filed copy against the row before moving on.
4. **What stays in `_raw/`.** Only originals that aren't filed: a file held or skipped (with the reason in the run log), a blank file, and a duplicate dropped where it can't be removed. New ones only come from `_imports/` and are moved to `_attachments/_raw/_imports/YYYY-MM-DD/`. Older ones, imported from home folders before 2026-09-28, kept their original filename and mirror their path under the home folder (`_attachments/_raw/Downloads/tom_payment_transition2.pdf`). Markdown kept in `_raw/` gets `.txt` added to its filename (`plan.md` → `plan.md.txt`; the bytes don't change, and the original name is the filename without `.txt`), so Obsidian and its plugins never read it as a note and its name can't clash with one; it never goes in a hidden folder, because Obsidian Sync doesn't carry those. Markdown originals from other vaults or exports go under `_attachments/_raw/_notes/<path>`; transcribe their content into the file that describes them instead of embedding. Filed capture notes move from `_captures/` to `_attachments/_raw/_captures/YYYY-MM-DD/` ([[#Capture notes]]). Older Markdown originals are still under the hidden `_attachments/_raw/.notes/` (Mac and git only). In `_raw/`, characters Obsidian Sync rejects on iOS and Windows (`: | " * ? < > \`, a newline, a trailing space or dot) become look-alikes (`:` → `꞉`, `|` → `ǀ`), each recorded in `_attachments/_raw/RENAMED-FOR-SYNC.tsv` (in Obsidian's Excluded files: out of search, still shown in the file explorer).
5. **Source table.** The file that describes it ends with a `## Source files` table mapping each vault file to its original path (its `_imports/…` path, or `_captures/…` for a capture note's attachment).
6. **Duplicates.** A file whose checksum is already in the manifest gets no second copy: add a manifest row with the existing `filed_path` and this `original_path`, and a row in the existing file's `## Source files`. A duplicate dropped in `_imports/` is then removed (Mac) or, where the importer can't delete another device, moved to `_attachments/_raw/_imports/YYYY-MM-DD/` and listed for [[= owner =]].

Never delete an original where it came from (home folders stay untouched). `_raw/` is never embedded and is exempt from the naming, placement, and orphan checks.

#### Allowed moves

The only moves of an original, and the only delete. Anything else, including an edit, is never allowed, except an erasure [[= owner =]] asks for directly ([[role-vault-docs]], Erase mode).

- A note from `_imports/` into `_captures/`.
- A file from `_imports/`, or a capture note's attachment from `_captures/`, into `_files/<note-slug>/` or `_attachments/<note-slug>/` (step 2).
[[% if store %]]
- A filed file from `_attachments/<note-slug>/` into `_files/<note-slug>/`, by `store-move` ([[#File store]]).
[[% endif %]]
- A filed capture note from `_captures/` into `_attachments/_raw/_captures/YYYY-MM-DD/`, as `<name>.md.txt`.
- A Markdown original into `_attachments/_raw/_notes/`, as `<name>.md.txt` (step 4).
- A duplicate drop into `_attachments/_raw/_imports/YYYY-MM-DD/`, where it can't be removed (step 6).
- **The one delete:** a duplicate drop on the Mac, after `cmp` confirms its bytes are already filed (step 6).
[[% if store %]]

### File store

Original files live outside the vault, so Sync and git stay small.

- **Where:** `[[= store_path =]]/` on the Mac, laid out as `<note-slug>/<file>`. The vault reaches it through `_files`, a symlink at the top of the vault. `_files` is git-ignored, in Sync's Excluded folders and in Obsidian's Excluded files. It exists only where the store is attached (the Mac). Everywhere else, a `_files/` embed shows as missing, and the note's summary carries the content.
- **What:** every filed attachment, images included, whatever the size. Images therefore don't show on the phone. `_attachments/_raw/` (the manifest, the Sync rename log, unfiled originals) stays in the vault.
- **Manifest:** a store file's row has `filed_path` `_files/<note-slug>/<file>`. The same rules apply: never edit, re-save, or delete a listed file; a rename or move updates its row.
- **Locked or unlocked:** the store is an encrypted disk image (`~/[[= store_path =]].sparsebundle`) mounted at `[[= store_path =]]`. `.scripts/store-image status` says `attached` or `locked`; `unlock` and `lock` are for [[= owner =]]. Locked, nothing writes to `_files/`, `store-move` refuses, and `vault-lint` skips the store checks as another device.
- **Moving files in:** `.scripts/store-move` (Mac only; `--dry-run` first, `--only <slug>` for one note). Per file, it copies, checks the checksum, repoints links in content notes, updates the manifest, and only then removes the vault copy. `vault-lint` gives one info line per note folder that still holds files that belong in the store.
[[% if backup %]]
- **Backup:** nightly restic snapshots of the store ([[vault-backup-and-restore]]). Git never holds store files.
[[% endif %]]
- **Private:** `_files/_private/` holds values waiting for the password manager: a PII register draft or `pii-register-queue.md`[[%+ if pii +%]] ([[sensitive-data]])[[%+ endif +%]]. Nothing embeds or links into it, it's never read into a report or chat, `vault-lint` skips it, and nothing in it is moved or deleted until [[= owner =]] says so.
- **Reading:** on the Mac, agents may read store files like any vault file. Write to the store only through the importer steps above, `store-move`, the [[procedures]] (Rename, Merge, Delete), and an erasure.
[[% endif %]]

### Capture notes

A capture note is one thing worth keeping, written for the [[role-vault-intake]] to file: a to-do, a fact, a project update, a question for [[= owner =]]. The format and the rules for writing one are in [[outside-agent-prompt]], the only copy: the [[role-vault-intake]] writes its notes in that format, and any outside agent gets that prompt pasted as is.

A note [[= owner =]] drops without the format's fields is filed the same way; the importer works out its Kind and home. Once filed, a capture note moves to `_attachments/_raw/_captures/YYYY-MM-DD/` as `<name>.md.txt` (Importing files, step 4), kept like any original; its attachments are filed like any imported file.

## Subfolders

Optional, at any depth, when a group of files is easier to browse together; most files sit directly in their folder. The rules are [[strict-rules]] I2 and I4. In addition:

- A file in a subfolder follows its top-level folder's anatomy as well as its schema.
- Name subfolders in lowercase kebab-case, like filenames.
- Before creating `Resources/fitness/plan.md`, search every folder for `plan.md`; pick a more specific title rather than reuse a name.
- `Agents/` has three fixed subfolders: `roles/` (`role-*`), `templates/` (`template-*`), and `logs/` (`log-*`). `vault-lint` errors on any of those kinds elsewhere in `Agents/`.
- Archiving moves a file to the top of `Archive/`, whatever subfolder it came from ([[procedures]]).
